VU1 x86-64 JIT: the block that ends an edge emits chain()'s exit/entry bookkeeping and jumps straight into the
successor (GOW_VU1_BLOCKCODE=1, off by default)

Every transition between two native VU1 blocks left the block through VU1NativeAccess::chain: a C++ call, the
successor's slot looked up again, the accounting, and emit()'s indirect jump into the successor. With
GOW_VU1_BLOCKCODE=1 the block that ends the edge emits that accounting itself, checks chain()'s conditions first, writes
the same values in the same order and jumps into the successor's body past its prologue, reusing the frame the chain
already shares. Nothing is written before the last check passes; a failed check jumps to the ordinary chainFn sequence
right below, which is the unmodified code, so the interpreter and the previous path stay as the fallback and the three
paths are semantically equivalent.

Left to chainFn: blocks whose first pair reads entry flags (their timing needs blockTiming), blocks that start an
FDIV/EFU operation (finishBlockTiming's scalarStart), self loops (chain()'s fastSelf), successors that are not x64 JIT
blocks, and GOW_VU1_OPSTAT (per-op statistics are counted in chain). VU1NativeEdgeOffsets (ps2_vu1_native.h) publishes
the layout the emitted code reads; edgeOffsets() fills it with offsetof() of the private NativeState / Slot /
VU1NativeCtx, so a layout change is a compile error and not silent corruption. The emitted code touches only the
registers both ABIs treat as volatile (rax, rcx, rdx, r8-r11): rdi and rsi are callee-saved on Windows x64 and
runNative / chainFn keep their state there.

Measured, ship deck combat, GOW_VU1N_X64=1 plus GOW_VU1_COST=1, 63 s, four runs per side, the same four 5 s windows
of t=45-60 s: vu_ms 4660.2 (4634.9 / 4667.3 / 4658.3 / 4680.5) -> 4631.3 (4612.3 / 4635.1 / 4648.9 / 4628.9), 0.6 %
less and inside the +/-1 % run-to-run band; chain_ms 1794 -> 1012 (-44 %) while block_ns 177.5 -> 185.2 (+4 %) because
the emitted edge is timed as part of the block; guest_flip_hz 16.0 -> 15.1 (noise). Bit-identical digest with the
switch on and off: x64 and x64-calls, 4 images / 12 trials, 8e2c2f39b854d8fa; with GOW_FUZZ_SPECIAL=1 (Inf/NaN)
9a9b264958dd8be2 both ways. anomalies=0 in every run.

Why this does not reach 25 fps: the transition was never the wall. chain() is 35-45 % of the block time, but the part
an inlined edge removes is one call, one slot lookup and one indirect jump - about 15-25 ns of the 59-87 ns per call;
the history entry, the tail record, commitDue and the lane masks remain, and the emitted edge pays for re-deriving the
same values from memory plus ~200 bytes per block (i-cache). The VU1 thread is busy 92-93 % of the wall (vu_ms 4.6 s
of every 5 s window) and ~90 % of that window is blockcode itself (entry 1.5-2.6 %, exit 0.5-1.5 %, dispatch 0-11 %),
so even a microprogram-wide compile that removed every entry, exit and dispatch could not gain the 1.6x that 25 fps
needs. Switch kept, default off, not in the default path.

